## Why This Is Asked
This tests multi-tenant governance, schema drift handling, lineage, and cost discipline across Azure data services.

## Key Concepts
- Event Hubs ingestion and Spark processing
- Tenant isolation via partitioning and ACLs
- Canonical schema and schema evolution
- Purview for lineage; Delta/Parquet for upserts
- Idempotent loads and per-tenant cost controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-Spark ingestion sketch
const df = spark.read.format("eventhubs").options({
  "eventhubs.connectionString": conn,
  "eventhubs.consumerGroup": "$Default"
}).load();

const parsed = df.selectExpr("CAST(body AS STRING) AS json")
  .select(from_json(col("json"), schema).alias("d")).select("d.*");

parsed.write.partitionBy("tenant_id").mode("append").parquet("<out_path>");
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you ensure schema evolution without breaking downstream?
- How would you monitor data lineage and quality across tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to design a minimal yet comprehensive Azure data engineering solution that handles event-driven ingestion, schema evolution, and enterprise data governance requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven ingestion using BlobCreated triggers
- Schema drift handling and unified schema inference
- Parquet conversion for optimized storage
- Incremental loading with MERGE operations
- Azure Purview integration for lineage tracking
- Data quality validation and error handling

